course: everyday
lesson: 232
title: Safe at lakes and beaches
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Open water is very different from a swimming pool. Lakes can be freezing even in summer, distances are hard to judge, and the sea has tides, waves and animals.
explain: The safest place to swim at a beach is where lifeguards watch. In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand that area is marked by red and yellow flags. In a boat or canoe you always wear a life jacket that fits.
explain: Many sea creatures are beautiful but should be left alone: some, like the cone shell and the blue-ringed octopus in Australia, can kill with their venom, and others, like crabs in rock pools, die if kept out of water.
explain: Example: a boy in Cornwall shuffles his feet as he walks in the shallow water. A weever fish buried in the sand feels him coming and swims off instead of stinging him.
fact: Cold water makes you gasp and tires your muscles fast, so get in slowly, stay near the shore and get out if you shiver.
fact: In a boat or canoe always wear a life jacket that fits and stay seated, because boats can tip over and a life jacket keeps you afloat even when you are tired or hurt.
fact: Blue-green scum on a lake can be poisonous algae, so do not swim and keep dogs out.
fact: Only swim as far as you can easily swim back, with a grown-up watching, because distances in water are hard to judge.
fact: At beaches with lifeguards, swim between the red and yellow flags, the area they watch.
fact: Keep sand holes no deeper than your knees and never dig tunnels, because sand collapses and can bury a person.
fact: Know the tide times; the rising sea can cut you off on rocks, sandbanks or around cliffs.
fact: Shuffle your feet and wear water shoes to warn weever fish and stingrays away; ease a sting in hot, but not scalding, water and get help.
fact: Where crocodiles may live, as in northern Australia, never swim and keep away from the water's edge.
fact: When a shark is sighted, get out of the water and follow the lifeguards.
fact: Put back shells with animals inside and rock pool creatures, because they need their homes and water.
fact: Never pick up a cone shell or blue-ringed octopus; they are small and pretty but deadly.
fact: Do not touch strange metal objects, drums or flares on the beach; tell a grown-up or lifeguard.
fact: Keep far away from seals and sea lions resting on the beach and keep dogs on a lead, because they can bite.
exercise: A lake feels freezing even though it is summer. What should you do? | Get in slowly, stay near the shore and get out if you shiver | Jump straight in all at once so your body gets used to it fast | Swim far out into the middle, where the water is always warmer | Stay in until you stop feeling the cold
exercise: Why is cold water dangerous for swimmers? | It makes you gasp and tires your muscles fast | It makes your body float far too high in the water | It always gives you a cold the next day | It is only dangerous if it is salty, like the sea
exercise: You are going out in a canoe on a calm lake. What must you wear? | A life jacket that fits | A warm, thick wool jumper | Armbands from the swimming pool | Nothing extra, since the lake is calm
exercise: There is green-blue scum on the lake and your dog wants to swim. What should you do? | Keep yourself and the dog out of the water | Let the dog swim but stay out yourself | Swim only where the scum looks thinnest and cleanest | Rinse the dog off with a bottle of water after it swims
exercise: Your friends dare you to swim to a raft far out on the lake. What should you think about? | Only swim as far as you can easily swim back | Rafts are always closer than they look | You can rest by floating on your back the whole way | Swimming back is easier than swimming out
exercise: You arrive at a beach in Australia with lifeguards. Where should you swim? | Between the red and yellow flags | Far from the flags where it is quiet | Next to the rocks where the waves are smaller | Anywhere, as lifeguards can see the whole beach
exercise: Your brother is digging a hole deeper than his waist. Why should he stop? | Sand holes can collapse and bury someone | The sea will fill it with fish | Deep holes are against beach rules only in winter | He will find nothing at that depth
exercise: The tide is coming in and you are exploring rocks around a cliff. What is the danger? | The rising sea could cut you off | The rocks will get too hot to stand on | The tide will bring sharks right in | The cliffs are only dangerous at night
exercise: How can you avoid stepping on a weever fish or stingray in shallow water? | Shuffle your feet and wear water shoes | Walk on tiptoe as quickly as you can | Splash loudly with your hands to scare them | Walk backwards into the sea
exercise: Your sister is stung on the foot by a weever fish. What helps the pain? | Put the foot in hot, not scalding, water and get help | Put ice on it and keep it there for a whole hour | Rub wet sand hard into the sting | Make her run up and down the beach to spread the venom out
exercise: You see a crocodile warning sign by a beach in Queensland. What should you do? | Stay out of the water and away from the edge | Swim quickly, staying close to the shore | Paddle only up to your knees, where crocodiles cannot reach | Swim, since crocodiles live only in rivers
exercise: The lifeguards whistle because a shark has been seen. What should you do? | Get out and follow the lifeguards' instructions | Swim out a little way to get a look at the shark | Stay in the shallow water, which is always safe from sharks | Splash to scare it away
exercise: You pick up a shell and see an animal inside. What should you do? | Put it back where you found it | Take it home to keep in a jar of water | Pull the animal out to clean the shell | Leave it in the sun on your towel
exercise: You find crabs and a starfish in a rock pool. What is right? | Look gently, handle them carefully if allowed, and put them back | Take them home in a bucket of seawater to keep in your bedroom | Leave them on a dry rock in the sun so you can watch them | Poke them with a stick to see if they move
exercise: You find a pretty little octopus with blue rings in a rock pool in Australia. What should you do? | Never touch it | Pick it up gently to show your family | Put it in your bucket | Stroke it with one finger
exercise: A rusty metal drum has washed up on the beach. What should you do? | Not touch it, and tell a grown-up or lifeguard | Open it carefully to see what is inside | Roll it back down the sand into the sea | Sit on it for a while to have a rest
exercise: A big seal is lying on the beach looking sleepy. What should you do? | Keep far away and keep your dog on a lead | Go up close to take a nice photo of it | Pour buckets of water on it to help it | Wake it gently so it goes back to sea
test: A girl in Ireland sees two beaches: one with lifeguards and red and yellow flags, one empty and quiet. Where is it safer to swim? | Between the flags at the lifeguarded beach | At the empty beach, as nobody is in the way | Either, as long as she swims fast | At the empty beach, near the rocks
test: A boy in Minnesota jumps straight into a very cold lake and gasps, swallowing water. What lesson should he learn? | Get into cold water slowly | Cold water is only dangerous in winter | Gasping means he is swimming well | He should have jumped in head first
test: A family finds a little cone shell with a lovely pattern in a rock pool in Western Australia. Why should they leave it? | Its sting can kill, even though it looks pretty | It is much too heavy for a child to carry | Shells start to smell bad once they are dried out at home | It is against the rules to take sand
test: Children on a sandbank notice the water rising around them. What should they have done? | Checked the tide times before going out | Brought a much bigger bucket to scoop the water away | Dug a deeper hole to stand in | Gone out even further, where the sand stays drier
test: Why is a tunnel in the sand more dangerous than a shallow castle moat? | The tunnel can collapse and bury a child | Tunnels fill up with seawater the instant they are dug | Moats are where the crabs live | Castles are protected from the sea by the tide
test: A boy finds an orange tube with writing on it in the seaweed. It might be a flare. What should he do? | Leave it and tell a lifeguard or grown-up | Hit it hard on a rock to get it open | Take it home to keep in his room as a souvenir | Throw it back in the sea
test: A girl wants to keep a crab from a rock pool in her bucket all afternoon. Why is that unkind? | It can die out of its home water | Crabs do not like the colour of buckets | Crabs will escape and bite her later | Keeping it is fine if she adds sand
test: Why do people on a boat trip need life jackets even if they can swim? | Boats tip over, and a life jacket keeps you afloat when tired or hurt | Life jackets keep you warm when you sit out in the sun | Only bad swimmers ever fall into the water from boats | They are just for show, so the photos look nice
test: A dog runs up to a sea lion resting on the beach. What could happen? | The sea lion could bite, as it is resting and wild | The sea lion will want to play chase with the dog | Nothing, because sea lions are always gentle with animals | The sea lion will go back to sea happily
test: lifeguard flags red n yellow over there, where do i swim | Between the red and yellow flags | Outside the flags where it's quiet | Anywhere you like | Next to the black flag
test: digging a massive hole, already up to my chest lol | Stop, keep it no deeper than your knees | Keep going, the deeper the better | Climb in and get ur mates to cover u up | Dig a tunnel out to the side as well
test: lake's got green scummy stuff on top, can the dog swim | No, it may be poisonous algae, keep the dog out | Yes, it's just weeds, they wash off | Yes, dogs can't get sick from lake water | Only if the dog swims fast
test: found a weird metal drum washed up, can i open it? | No, don't touch it, tell a grown-up or lifeguard | Yes, it might have treasure inside | Yes, if you poke the lid off with a long stick | Roll it back into the sea
explain: A boy of nine swam out past the flags at Bondi; the rip pulled him out and lifeguards could not reach him in time.
explain: A girl in Michigan swam out to a raft in a lake; halfway she got tired and cold and went under, and a man in a boat pulled her out just in time.
fact: Drowning is quick and silent: a drowning child often cannot shout or wave.
fact: A rip is a strong current that pulls swimmers out to sea; if one catches you, float, stay calm, raise a hand and swim sideways, along the beach, not straight back.
fact: Lakes are colder and deeper than they look, and swimmers can get tired far from shore.
test: swim past the flags? water's calm | No, always swim between the flags | Yes, if it's calm that's fine | Yes, the lifeguard will still see u out there | Only for a minute
test: caught in a rip, it's pulling me out | Float, stay calm, raise a hand and swim sideways | Swim straight back to shore as hard as you can | Dive under to escape the pull | Take off your swimsuit so you're lighter n faster
test: Why do many children drown with people nearby? | Drowning is quick and silent, with no shouting or waving | Drowning children always splash and scream loudly for help | It only happens when the lifeguard is on a break | Children only ever drown in very deep water far away from people
test: did u hear a kid dug a massive hole at the beach and it caved in on him and they had to dig him out? real lesson? | Keep sand holes no deeper than your knees, no tunnels | Dig only in wet sand, because wet sand never falls in | Dig fast before it caves in | Only grown-ups can get buried by sand, kids are too light
test: apparently a dog swam in the green lake and died that same night, everyone at the dog park's saying it. true lesson? | Blue-green scum can be poisonous; keep out and keep dogs out | Lakes are always safe for dogs, whatever colour the water | Only brown water is dangerous | The dog must have been ill already before it went in the water
test: my mate says when someone's drowning they scream and splash like in the films so u'd definitely notice. right? | No, drowning is often quick and silent | Yes, they always shout for help really loudly | Yes, and they wave both arms above their heads | Yes, so you don't really need to watch them
exam: Identify the area of a beach patrolled by lifeguards in which one is to swim. | Between the red and yellow flags | Outside the flags, where it is quieter | Near the rocks at the far end | Wherever the waves are largest
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appearance of a drowning child? | The child is often silent and unable to shout or wave | The child invariably screams and splashes in a manner none could miss | The child waves both arms above the head | The child calls out repeatedly and loudly for help from the shore
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice upon seeing a strange metal drum or flare on the beach. | It is not touched, and an adult or lifeguard is informed | It is opened to inspect its contents | It is thrown back into the sea | It is carried home as a souvenir
exam: Ascertain the reason for which cold water is entered slowly. | Cold water causes gasping and tires the muscles rapidly | Cold water causes the skin to grow warmer | Slow entry makes the water itself grow warmer around the swimmer | Quick entry is forbidden by lifeguards on all public beaches
